\f0\fs40 \cf2 Refractive laser surgery is used to reduce dependence on glasses or contact lenses by reshaping the cornea, the clear front window of the eye. Two common options are laser-assisted in situ keratomileusis and photorefractive keratectomy.\
\
In laser-assisted in situ keratomileusis, a thin flap is created in the cornea, the laser reshapes the tissue underneath, and the flap is placed back down. Vision usually improves quickly, often within a day or two, and discomfort is usually mild. The main advantages are faster visual recovery and less early discomfort.\
\
In photorefractive keratectomy, there is no flap. Instead, the surface layer is removed and the laser treats the front of the cornea directly. This avoids flap-related problems and may be preferred in some patients, especially when corneal thickness, lifestyle, or anatomy make a flap less ideal. The trade-off is a slower recovery. Early discomfort is greater, and useful vision often takes several days to improve, with continued healing over weeks.\
\
Both procedures can work very well in appropriate candidates, but neither guarantees perfect vision or complete freedom from glasses. Risks can include dry eye symptoms, glare, halos, under-correction, over-correction, regression, infection, inflammation, and in rare cases loss of best-corrected vision. Careful screening is important to reduce risk.\
\
Good candidates need healthy eyes, stable prescriptions, and realistic expectations. Your surgeon will help decide which option, if any, is safest and most appropriate for you.}